    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Is This a Good Offer?\n\n**Yes, this is an exceptional, top-decile offer for a Google L6 Senior Product Manager.**\n\n* **Base Salary ($260,000):** Sits near the top ceiling of the posted range ($183,000\u2013$271,000).\n* **Sign-On Bonus ($200,000):** Well above standard Google L6 sign-on amounts (typically $50,000\u2013$100,000). It makes you completely whole for the $200,000 in Meta equity you forfeit over the next 12 months.\n* **Equity ($1,340,000 / 4 years = $335,000/yr average):** Far above median L6 PM equity grants at Google (which typically hover around $200,000\u2013$240,000/yr).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Compensation Comparison: Meta IC6 vs. Google L6\n\n#### A. Annualized Comparison (Recurring Baseline)\n| Component | Meta IC6 (Current) | Google L6 (Offer) | Difference |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $260,000 | +$20,000 |\n| **Target Bonus** | $36,000 (15%) | $52,000 (20%) | +$16,000 |\n| **RSUs (Annual Avg)**| ~$250,000 | $335,000 | +$85,000 |\n| **Total Annualized** | **~$526,000** | **~$647,000** | **+$121,000 / yr (+23%)** |\n\n#### B. First-Year Cash Flow & Vesting Nuance\nGoogle typically utilizes a **front-loaded vesting schedule** (commonly 38% / 32% / 20% / 10% or 33% / 33% / 22% / 12%):\n\n* **Year 1 Total Comp (approx. 38% vesting):**\n  * Base: $260,000\n  * Bonus: $52,000\n  * Year 1 Equity (38% of $1.34M): ~$509,200\n  * Sign-on Bonus: $200,000\n  * **Total Year 1 Earnings: ~$1,021,200** *(vs. ~$526,000 at Meta)*\n* Even on an annualized/equal-vesting basis (25%/yr), Year 1 is **$847,000**.\n\n#### C. The Equity Cliff & Refreshers\nBecause Google front-loads new-hire grants, your initial grant pays out significantly less in Years 3 and 4. Google addresses this through **annual equity refreshers**, which stack on top of your existing vests. Given that you will be starting with an above-band initial grant, maintaining this payout in Years 3\u20134 will rely on solid performance ratings to secure healthy refresher grants.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Accept, Negotiate, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** Absolutely not. This role aligns with your top choice, provides an immediate career move from Meta to Google at the same level, and represents a massive compensation jump.\n* **Accept As-Is vs. Negotiate?** You are in a strong position, but because you do not have competing offers and Google already offered top-band numbers, you must handle negotiation tactfully to avoid friction.\n\n**Recommendation:** Negotiate with a **single, highly collaborative \"Commitment to Sign\" counter-offer.**\n\nGoogle recruiters appreciate candidates who offer a clean path to closing without prolonged back-and-forth.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What to Ask For and How Much\n\nGiven that the base is nearly at the posting cap and the sign-on is already maxed out:\n\n1. **Primary Target \u2014 Equity:** Ask to round the equity grant from **$1,340,000 to $1,450,000** (or $1,500,000). Equity has the most discretion at Google compensation committees.\n2. **Alternative Target \u2014 Base:** Ask for **$270,000** (the top of the posted $271k band).\n\n#### Strategy: The \"Closing Lever\"\nFrame the ask around giving you 100% certainty to sign immediately without exploring any other options.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Negotiation Script / Email\n\n> *\"Hi [Recruiter Name],*\n>\n> *Thank you again for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve really enjoyed getting to know [Hiring Manager / Team Name] and the team, and Google is unequivocally my #1 choice. I\u2019m genuinely excited about the impact I can have in this role.*\n>\n> *I reviewed the compensation package carefully. The structure is very strong, and I appreciate the team putting their best foot forward.*\n>\n> *Because leaving my current scope and vesting trajectory at Meta is a big step, if we can bridge the equity grant to **$1,450,000** (or bring base to **$270,000**), I am ready to sign the offer immediately today and wrap up the process.*\n>\n> *Can we see if the compensation committee can approve that adjustment? Either way, I\u2019m thrilled about the opportunity and look forward to working together.\"*\n\n---\n\n### What to Expect Next\n* **If they say yes:** You gain an additional $110,000 in equity and lock in an outstanding package.\n* **If they say no:** Google will not rescind an offer for a polite, professional counter-offer tied to an immediate commitment to sign. If they hold firm at $1.34M, you can enthusiastically accept knowing you already secured a top-percentile offer.",
